    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Let's focus on the mathematical aspect of calculating safety stock levels for your inventory management.\n\nTo calculate safety stock, you can use the following formula:\n\nSafety Stock = (Average Daily Usage * Lead Time) + (Demand Variability * Lead Time)\n\nWhere:\n- Average Daily Usage is the average number of lithium-ion battery cells you use per day.\n- Lead Time is the average time it takes for your Shenzhen suppliers to deliver the parts, in days.\n- Demand Variability is the standard deviation of your daily usage, which accounts for fluctuations in demand.\n\nTo calculate demand variability, you can use historical data to find the standard deviation of your daily usage. Once you have that, you can adjust the safety stock level to account for potential fluctuations in demand.\n\nFor example, if your average daily usage is 100 cells, the lead time is 10 days, and the standard deviation of your daily usage is 20 cells, your safety stock level would be:\n\nSafety Stock = (100 * 10) + (20 * 10) = 1,200 cells\n\nThis means you should keep a safety stock of 1,200 lithium-ion battery cells to cover the lead time and account for demand variability.\n\nI hope this",
