      "content": "Ah, dealing with the unpredictable nature of goblin-like disruptions in your supply chain can indeed be challenging. To account for these random delays, you might consider using a more robust approach to your safety stock calculation, which includes a higher level of uncertainty.\n\nOne method is to use a service level approach, where you determine the probability of not running out of stock (the service level) and then calculate the safety stock needed to achieve that service level. This method takes into account the random nature of delays and helps you decide how much extra stock you should keep on hand.\n\nFor example, if you want to have a 95% service level, you would look up the corresponding z-score in a standard normal distribution table, which is approximately 1.65 for a 95% service level. Then, you would calculate your safety stock using the following formula:\n\nSafety Stock = z-score * Standard Deviation of Lead Time Demand\n\nWhere the standard deviation of lead time demand is calculated by multiplying the standard deviation of daily usage by the square root of the lead time.\n\nIf your standard deviation of daily usage is 20 cells, and your lead time is 10 days, the standard deviation of lead time demand would be:\n\nStandard Deviation of Lead Time Demand",
